<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juscelino Kubitschek</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1902">1902</a>–<a href="/years/1976">1976</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juscelino Kubitschek was a doctor and politician, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a>, who served as president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> from 1956 to 1961. Promising 'fifty years in five', he pushed rapid industrialisation, road building and the motor industry. His most famous project was the new capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brasilia">Brasília</a>, inaugurated in 1960, though his spending fed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> and foreign debt.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juscelino-kubitschek.jpg" alt="Juscelino Kubitschek" width="320" height="478" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Governo do Brasil · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juscelino_Kubitschek_in_1956.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kubitschek was born on 12 September 1902 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diamantina">Diamantina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a>, into a poor family. His father, a travelling salesman, died when he was two, and his mother Júlia, a schoolteacher, raised him. He graduated in medicine in 1927 and later specialised in urology in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. He was elected a federal deputy in 1934 but lost the seat when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estado-novo-portugal">Estado Novo</a> began in 1937. In 1940 he became mayor of Belo Horizonte, where he began his working relationship with the architect Oscar Niemeyer, and in 1950 he became governor of Minas Gerais.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Kubitschek campaigned in 1955 with the slogan of fifty years of progress in five and won the October election with 35.6 per cent of the vote, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joao-goulart">João Goulart</a> as his running mate. Opponents pressed to annul the result, and General Henrique Teixeira Lott led a military countercoup to secure his inauguration on 31 January 1956. His Goals Plan had 31 goals in six groups, with the construction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brasilia">Brasília</a> as the main one. Imported machinery was exempted from taxes where foreign capital was associated with national capital, and the motor industry grew. By the end of his term foreign debt had risen from 87 million to 297 million dollars, industrial production had grown by 80 per cent and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> stood at 43 per cent.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brasilia">Brasília</a> had been envisaged in earlier constitutions, but planning began only in 1956. Lúcio Costa, Oscar Niemeyer and Roberto Burle Marx led the work, which started in February 1957 and brought some 30,000 workers to the savanna. The city was inaugurated by the target date of 21 April 1960. His government was often accused of corruption, particularly over the construction.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Kubitschek was elected senator for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goias-velho">Goiás</a> in 1961. After the military coup of 1964 he was accused of corruption and of communist support, his mandate was revoked and his political rights were suspended for ten years. He lived in voluntary exile in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> in March 1967. He died on 22 August 1976 in a car crash near Resende, and some 350,000 mourners attended his burial in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brasilia">Brasília</a>, where he lies in the JK Memorial.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Juscelino Kubitschek” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Juscelino Kubitschek</b> promised 'fifty years in five' and built the new capital, Brasília, inaugurated in 1960.</blockquote><a href="/state/fourth-brazilian-republic">Fourth Brazilian Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Diamantina, in what is now <a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a>. Died in Resende, in what is now <a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-18.25,-43.6&amp;year=1950" aria-label="Diamantina on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3368 2840 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Juscelino Kubitschek"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3818 3110h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3818 3110h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-18.25,-43.6&amp;year=1950">What was here in 1950? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
